  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "A date as text takes at most two digits for month and day",
  "statement": "In a three-part numeric date, leading zeros are not free. VALUE(\"2024/02/01\") is 45323, but VALUE(\"2024/02/001\") and VALUE(\"2024/002/01\") are #VALUE!, and so is any month or day of three or more digits in the 45 forms read. A year of one, two or four digits is read: \"1/2/1\" and \"1/02/01\" are 2001-02-01, \"24/2/1\" is 2024-02-01, while \"0001/2/1\" is #VALUE!. Month 0 and day 0 give #VALUE!. The same limit holds with hyphens: \"2024-02-001\" through VALUE, and \"2024-002-01\" and \"2024-02-001\" through DATEVALUE, are #VALUE!. The settings of this run are en-ZA: decimal point, space as thousands separator, year-month-day date order.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "45 recorded VALUE readings covering year, month and day tokens of one to five digits, and 2 DATEVALUE readings.",
  "inferred": null,
